ntdll: fall back to the installed libd3dshared.dylib when the variable is unset.

Wine only registers a non-native (dynamically generated x86_64) code region with
Rosetta if CX_APPLEGPTK_LIBD3DSHARED_PATH points at Apple's libd3dshared.dylib.
CrossOver's launcher sets that on every run; nothing else does, and a process whose
environment was composed by an application never inherits it. Battle.net's Agent.exe
builds its own environment block for the game it launches, so starting Diablo IV from
the client's Play button gives a game with no registration: diablo_iv_loader.dll
generates code at runtime, drives it with fibers plus SetThreadContext, and without the
registration the fiber switch does not return where it expects, its scheduler loop
re-enters, and it deadlocks re-acquiring its own non-recursive SRW lock. The symptom is
a process at 0.0% CPU with 122 MB resident and nine threads, no window, and not one byte
in the game's own _FenrisDebug-*.txt.

Resolving the library relative to dll_dir removes the dependency on the environment, so
the Play button works. The variable still wins when set, which keeps CrossOver's
behaviour as an override. dll_dir is <prefix>/lib/wine and is NULL only in a build tree,
where there is no installed library to fall back to, so behaviour there is unchanged --
as it is when the file is absent, since dlopen then fails exactly as it does today.

The two TRACEs now name the path they used. Whether the variable arrived or the fallback
ran is precisely the question this patch exists to answer, and WINEDEBUG=+module is how
it gets answered.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c
@@ -1321,13 +1321,30 @@
 static void init_non_native_support(void)
 {
     char *libd3dshared_path = getenv( "CX_APPLEGPTK_LIBD3DSHARED_PATH" );
+    char *default_path = NULL;
 
     register_non_native_code_region = NULL;
     supports_non_native_code_regions = NULL;
 
-    if (!libd3dshared_path || !sonoma_or_later())
+    if (!sonoma_or_later())
         return;
 
+    /* Fall back to the libd3dshared.dylib installed beside D3DMetal.framework, so that
+     * the registration below stops depending on a variable reaching this process.
+     * CrossOver's launcher exports CX_APPLEGPTK_LIBD3DSHARED_PATH on every run, but a
+     * process whose environment an application composed never receives it: Battle.net's
+     * Agent.exe builds its own block for the game it launches, so Diablo IV starts
+     * without the variable, no code region is ever registered, and its protected loader
+     * self-deadlocks re-acquiring a non-recursive SRW lock -- no window, and not a byte
+     * in the game's own log. dll_dir is <prefix>/lib/wine, and is NULL only when running
+     * from a build tree, where there is no installed library to fall back to.
+     */
+    if (!libd3dshared_path && dll_dir)
+        libd3dshared_path = default_path = build_path( dll_dir, "../external/libd3dshared.dylib" );
+
+    if (!libd3dshared_path)
+        return;
+
     non_native_support_lib = dlopen( libd3dshared_path, RTLD_LOCAL );
     if (non_native_support_lib)
     {
@@ -1345,11 +1362,13 @@
             libd3dshared_code_end = (char *)libd3dshared_load_addr + code_size;
         }
 
-        TRACE( "Loaded libd3dshared.dylib, does%s support non-native code regions\n",
+        TRACE( "Loaded %s, does%s support non-native code regions\n", libd3dshared_path,
                 supports_non_native_code_regions ? (supports_non_native_code_regions() ? "" : " not") : " not" );
     }
     else
-        TRACE( "Loading libd3dshared.dylib failed: %s\n", dlerror() );
+        TRACE( "Loading %s failed: %s\n", libd3dshared_path, dlerror() );
+
+    free( default_path );
 }
 
 static NTSTATUS pe_module_loaded( void *args )
